\ The exact interval preceding or following each gate of the cutlets.
\ For positive n the sauce input is (Foundation, Foundation+n).
\ For negative -n it is (Foundation, Foundation-n).
\ Ask fixed bowl 1 with seal 1, choose 1..922 and add 41.
\ The permitted interval is 42..963 days.
\ This NATIVE gate-index adapter is restricted to +/-400000000.
\ Zero is not a valid gate gap, and out-of-range requests return
\ interval length zero rather than overflowing native arithmetic.
\ The independent wide gate-index implementation is in its own file.

A gate inquiry has
  a number called signed index,
  a number called interval length.

The gate inquiry is a gate inquiry.

\ The signed index must not be zero. Zero is Foundation itself.
\ Converting day positions to full wide counters is a future requirement.
To calculate native gate spacing given a number:
  Put 0 into the gate inquiry's interval length.
  Put the number into the gate inquiry's signed index.
  If the number is 0, exit.
  If the number is greater than 400000000, exit.
  If the number is less than -400000000, exit.
  Put -15055671 into the example's calculation day.
  Put -15055671 into the example's requested day.
  Add the number to the example's requested day.
  Answer the example.
  Copy the example into the sauce input.
  Make the complete sauce.
  Select the sealed gate interval.

\ Both the native-index and signed-wide-index gate routines use the
\ identical canonical bowl 1 / seal 1 choice. This is deliberately
\ separate from constructing the five counters.
To select the sealed gate interval:
  Put 1 into the seal inquiry's bowl identity.
  Put 1 into the seal inquiry's seal number.
  Begin a sealed inquiry.
  Put "922" into an interval options wide quantity.
  Choose an equal option given the interval options wide quantity.
  Convert the seal inquiry's selected quantity to a chosen interval number.
  Add 41 to the chosen interval number.
  Put the chosen interval number into the gate inquiry's interval length.
